I have a mutation that I want to catch if the procedure throws an error, I'd like to get the code of the error ("UNAUTHORIZED", "FORBIDDEN" etc.) but there are no types on TRCPClientError with the property code. I saw a generic that you can pass to the error but I don't know what to pass as the generic to get intelisense.
```ts
try {
    await mutateAsync(id);
} catch (error) {
    if(error instanceof TRPCClientError<>){
        //Process the error here if unauthorized regenerate accessToken and retry
    }
}
```
Is there any way to do so?

You can do this via the `errorFormatter` when you initialize tRPC:

```ts
const t = initTRPC.create({
  errorFormatter: ({ shape, error }) => {
    return {
      ...shape,
      code: error.code, // UNAUTHORIZED | INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR | BAD_REQUEST ...
    };
  },
});
```

Then you'll have it available on the client:

```ts
export default function IndexPage() {
  const result = trpc.greeting.useQuery({ name: 'client' });

  const error = result.error;
  error?.shape?.errorCode;

  return ( ... );
}
```

Hey there 👋

I have the following scenario:
two middlewares, one for authentication, one for other stuff but relies on the auth middleware to set the context.

```ts
export const authMiddleware = t.middleware(async ({ ctx, next, meta }) => {
	const session = await getSession(ctx.req)

	const identity = getIdentity(session)

	if (!identity.tenantId) {
		throw new TRPCError({ code: "UNAUTHORIZED", message: "You are not a member of a registered Tenant" })
	}

	if (meta.every) {
		requireAllPermissions(identity, meta.every)
	}
	if (meta.some) {
		requireOnePermission(identity, meta.some)
	}

	return next({
		ctx: {
			...ctx,
			identity: { ...identity } as Identity,
		},
	})
})
```

and another one where `identity` does not exist on the context.

```ts
export const anotherMiddleware = t.middleware(async ({ ctx, next, meta, rawInput }) => {
	const userId = ctx.identity.id // identity does not exist on context
}
```

Is there a way to specify the context type for middlewares? I tried looking at the generics of the middleware function but could not figure it out.

Chaining it like this also does not work because I want to use "anotherMiddleware" in several places but the chaining returns a procedure, not a middleware.

```ts
const anotherMiddleware = t.procedure.use(authMiddleware).use(({ ctx, next }) => {
         // ctx.identity is defined but this returns a procedure and not a middleware
	return next()
})
```

What I have done now is to create another "trpc"-object with a more defined Context type.

```ts
export const anotherT = initTRPC
	.context<AugmentedContext>() // AugmentedContext defines the identity to be defined
		transformer: superjson,
	})
```

If I now define the middleware based on `anotherT`, the types work but I have to make sure that the `authMiddleware` always run before the the `anotherMiddleware`. This is easy enough to ensure, but I was wondering whether this would be considered ok to do or whether there are any gotchas about having multiple "trpc"-objects.

```ts
export const anotherMiddleware = anotherT.middleware(async ({ ctx, next, meta, rawInput }) => {
	const userId = ctx.identity.id // identity does exist on context
}
```

Any help is much appreciated 🙏
